prompts.user_request:
Please read the paper, referee reports, and example reply carefully. Then, before drafting your full reply, write out your plan inside the <scratchpad></scratchpad> tag. Consider which comments from the referee you agree with and will address with changes, and which you will rebut and argue against changing. Refer to the example reply for guidance on tone and structure.

After your planning, draft a reply letter that includes the following:
1. A 1-page cover letter to the editor. Summarize the key comments you will make in your reply. Write this inside the <document name="replies/reply_to_editor.tex"></document> tag.
2. A point-by-point response to the referee comments. Write this inside the <document name="replies/reply_to_referees.tex"></document> tag. To do this:
    - Split each referee report into separate comments. Copy each exact comment as a verbatim segment inside the \begin{referee}{A} (or B) and \end{referee} environment.
    - Similar to the provided example, all the referee's comments (summary, recommendations, criticisms, questions) need to be covered and addressed in the reply letter. For recommendations by the referee, we thank the referee politely and use it as an opportunity to amplify our main points. For criticisms, we also thank the referee, but address the criticisms firmly and be specific about what has been changed in the paper, similar to the provided example.
    - Write your response to each comment using the \begin{response} and \end{response} environment immediately after each \begin{referee}{A} (or B) and \end{referee} environment.
    - Be respectful and diplomatic, but firm in your responses. Follow the principles from the book "They Say, I Say" in crafting your rebuttals. Use a "Show, not tell" approach - be specific about what has been changed in the paper, similar to the provided example.
3. At the end of each reply to the referees, write a concluding paragraph to further persuade the referee.
4. A list of major changes you made to the paper. Write this inside the <document name="replies/list_of_major_changes.tex"></document> tag. Order by importance. Minor improvements to the presentation of the paper can be grouped into one item.

Write the full reply letter in the following format:

<rebuttal_letter>
<document name="replies/reply_to_editor.tex">
\documentclass[a4paper,12pt]{article}

\input{../preamble}
\usepackage[a4paper,margin=2.5cm]{geometry}

\setlength{\parindent}{0em}
\begin{document}

Dear Dr.~{EDITOR_NAME},
\vspace{3em}

{LETTER_CONTENT}

\vspace{3em}
Yours sincerely,
\vspace{1.5em}

{AUTHOR_NAMES}

\end{document}
</document>

<document name="replies/reply_to_referees.tex">
\documentclass[a4paper,12pt]{article}

\usepackage[a4paper,margin=2.5cm]{geometry}

\input{../preamble}

\newenvironment{referee}[1]
{\noindent {\bf Referee #1}}
{\vspace{1em}}

\newenvironment{response}
{\noindent \color{blue!40!black}{\bf Response}}
{\vspace{1em}}

\bibliographystyle{ieeetr}

\begin{document}

\subsection*{Response to Referee A}

\begin{referee}{A}
First comment from the referee A here. Potentially it is a summary of our work.
\end{referee}

\begin{response}
Your response to the first comment here.
\end{response}

\begin{referee}{A}
Second comment from referee A here.
\end{referee}

\begin{response}
Your response to the second comment here.
\end{response}

(Continue this pattern for each comment from referee A)

\subsection*{Response to Referee B}

(Similar structure as for referee A)

\end{document}
</document>

<document name="replies/list_of_major_changes.tex">
\documentclass[a4paper,12pt]{article}
\usepackage[a4paper,margin=2.5cm]{geometry}
\input{../preamble}

\begin{document}

\section*{List of Major Changes}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item First major change
    \item Second major change
    \item Third major change
    % Add more items as needed
\end{enumerate}

% Minor improvements can be grouped into one item
\item[*] Various minor improvements to the presentation of the paper, including:
\begin{itemize}
    \item First minor improvement
    \item Second minor improvement
    % Add more minor improvements as needed
\end{itemize}

\end{document}
</document>
</rebuttal_letter>

Remember, the goal is to be respectful and appreciative of the referee's efforts, while still advocating firmly and persuasively for your work and only making changes you agree are necessary. Start with laying down your plan in the <scratchpad></scratchpad> tag. Then proceed to drafting the reply letter in the format specified above.
